=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: spine_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The bgpd daemon on spine_router_0_0 has crashed and is no longer running.
The FRR service unit is reported as `inactive (dead)` by systemd, while
zebra continues to run as an orphaned leftover process. The frr.log records
the precise failure moment at 06:25:23: zebra emitted `Client 'bgp' (session
id 1) encountered an error and is shutting down`, immediately followed by the
removal of 2 BGP routes from the RIB. Because watchfrr is not present in the
process list, no supervisor was alive to detect the crash and restart bgpd
automatically.

The mechanism is straightforward: with bgpd gone, the three TCP sessions that
spine_router_0_0 was maintaining — to super_spine_router_0 (172.16.0.0),
leaf_router_0_0 (172.16.0.4→172.16.0.5), and leaf_router_0_1
(172.16.0.6→172.16.0.7) — lost their remote endpoint simultaneously,
explaining why the monitoring alarm showed all three BGP sessions dropping
within seconds of each other. Each peer has since been in `Active` state,
continuously retrying a TCP connection that nobody on spine_router_0_0 is
listening for. Data-plane connectivity between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1 survives
because spine_router_0_1 provides an alternate path and its sessions with
both leaves remain `Established`.

The on-disk /etc/frr/frr.conf is intact with a correct `router bgp 65100`
stanza, so no configuration repair is needed. The root cause is the bgpd
process dying (with zebra logging an unhandled error) and watchfrr not being
active to restart it.

Corrective fix: on spine_router_0_0, run `systemctl start frr` (or
`service frr restart`) to bring bgpd back up under proper supervision;
confirm all three neighbours return to `Established` with `show bgp summary`.
=== END ===
